  "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,\u21d2\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{x}^{2/3}}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5{c}_{1}}{6} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{5}\\,\u21d2\\,{c}_{2}=-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$",
